1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,392
Laddoo Lab sells a six-pack for
a hundred and twenty rupees.

2
00:00:04,692 --> 00:00:06,709
But where does that number come from?

3
00:00:06,709 --> 00:00:09,062
Is it too high, too low, or just right?

4
00:00:09,412 --> 00:00:13,023
There are three ways to check
it: cost, competition, and value.

5
00:00:13,673 --> 00:00:18,354
In Nani's kitchen, Zoya opens her
notebook, and Arjun brings the bills.

6
00:00:18,704 --> 00:00:23,416
Nani lists what goes into every pack:
ragi, jaggery, ghee, and the box.

7
00:00:23,766 --> 00:00:26,504
Let's test our price
three ways, Zoya says.

8
00:00:27,154 --> 00:00:30,581
First, cost-based pricing,
also called cost-plus.

9
00:00:30,881 --> 00:00:34,446
Add up what one pack costs
to make, then add a markup.

10
00:00:34,446 --> 00:00:37,672
A markup is the extra you
add on top, as profit.

11
00:00:38,322 --> 00:00:39,507
Let's build it.

12
00:00:39,507 --> 00:00:43,219
The ingredients for one
six-pack cost forty five rupees.

13
00:00:43,569 --> 00:00:45,631
The box and label, fifteen.

14
00:00:45,631 --> 00:00:47,631
Gas and electricity, eight.

15
00:00:47,631 --> 00:00:48,992
Delivery, twelve.

16
00:00:49,342 --> 00:00:51,698
Total cost: eighty rupees a pack.

17
00:00:52,048 --> 00:00:54,940
Laddoo Lab adds a markup of fifty percent.

18
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:56,728
Half of eighty is forty.

19
00:00:57,078 --> 00:01:00,252
Eighty plus forty is a
hundred and twenty rupees.

20
00:01:00,252 --> 00:01:01,917
The cost test says yes.

21
00:01:02,567 --> 00:01:04,692
Second, competition-based pricing.

22
00:01:04,692 --> 00:01:07,792
Look at what rivals charge
for something similar.

23
00:01:08,142 --> 00:01:10,818
A bakery's laddoo box sells for a hundred.

24
00:01:10,818 --> 00:01:13,734
A supermarket snack
pack, a hundred and ten.

25
00:01:13,734 --> 00:01:16,996
A health store's millet
bars, a hundred and thirty.

26
00:01:17,346 --> 00:01:20,803
So rivals sit between a hundred
and a hundred and thirty.

27
00:01:20,803 --> 00:01:23,027
A hundred and twenty fits right in.

28
00:01:23,677 --> 00:01:25,366
Third, value-based pricing.

29
00:01:25,366 --> 00:01:29,508
Ask what the pack is worth to the
customer, not what it costs you.

30
00:01:29,858 --> 00:01:33,600
Zoya asks ten parents: what
would a six-pack be worth to you?

31
00:01:33,950 --> 00:01:38,655
Their answers range from a hundred and
twenty to a hundred and sixty rupees.

32
00:01:39,005 --> 00:01:41,183
The average is a hundred and forty.

33
00:01:41,183 --> 00:01:44,991
So at a hundred and twenty,
parents feel they get a good deal.

34
00:01:45,641 --> 00:01:47,119
Cost sets the floor.

35
00:01:47,119 --> 00:01:48,745
Value sets the ceiling.

36
00:01:49,445 --> 00:01:51,781
Kotler and Armstrong say it simply.

37
00:01:51,781 --> 00:01:53,662
Costs set the lowest price.

38
00:01:53,662 --> 00:01:55,790
Customer value sets the highest.

39
00:01:56,140 --> 00:02:00,409
For Laddoo Lab, the floor is eighty
rupees, and the ceiling is a hundred

40
00:02:00,409 --> 00:02:01,309
and forty.

41
00:02:01,550 --> 00:02:04,469
Rival prices help pick a spot in between.

42
00:02:04,819 --> 00:02:07,643
A hundred and twenty
passes all three tests.

43
00:02:07,643 --> 00:02:10,574
So the six-pack stays
at a hundred and twenty.

44
00:02:11,224 --> 00:02:13,962
Where do we see these three
methods at work today?

45
00:02:14,312 --> 00:02:15,212
Cost-based.

46
00:02:15,138 --> 00:02:19,448
A furniture maker adds a fixed
markup to the cost of each table.

47
00:02:19,798 --> 00:02:20,978
Competition-based.

48
00:02:20,978 --> 00:02:24,803
Two tea stalls on the same
street charge almost the same.

49
00:02:25,153 --> 00:02:26,082
Value-based.

50
00:02:26,082 --> 00:02:29,913
An airline charges more for
a seat with extra legroom.

51
00:02:30,563 --> 00:02:32,413
But is any method perfect?

52
00:02:32,413 --> 00:02:34,324
Each one has a blind spot.

53
00:02:34,674 --> 00:02:36,712
Cost-plus ignores the customer.

54
00:02:36,712 --> 00:02:39,877
Copying rivals can start
a race to the bottom.

55
00:02:39,877 --> 00:02:41,977
And value needs real research.

56
00:02:42,327 --> 00:02:46,742
Dev says, parents value it at a hundred
and forty, so let's charge that.

57
00:02:47,092 --> 00:02:48,157
Zoya says no.

58
00:02:48,157 --> 00:02:52,163
A small gap below value keeps
parents happy, and coming back.

59
00:02:52,513 --> 00:02:57,712
And in India, every pack must print
its M R P, the maximum retail price,

60
00:02:57,712 --> 00:02:59,245
including all taxes.

61
00:02:59,245 --> 00:03:01,121
No shop may charge more.

62
00:03:01,471 --> 00:03:02,371
The lesson?

63
00:03:02,322 --> 00:03:06,306
Know your cost, watch your rivals,
and price by value, fairly.

64
00:03:06,956 --> 00:03:07,922
Quick check.

65
00:03:07,922 --> 00:03:11,410
Zoya adds fifty percent to
her cost of eighty rupees.

66
00:03:11,410 --> 00:03:12,945
Which method is this?

67
00:03:14,845 --> 00:03:16,128
Cost-based pricing!

68
00:03:16,128 --> 00:03:19,249
Cost plus a markup gives
a hundred and twenty.

69
00:03:19,849 --> 00:03:20,858
So, remember.

70
00:03:21,158 --> 00:03:24,133
Cost-based: add up your
costs, then add a markup.

71
00:03:24,483 --> 00:03:27,821
Competition-based: price
close to what rivals charge.

72
00:03:28,171 --> 00:03:31,779
Value-based: price by what the
customer feels it is worth.

73
00:03:32,379 --> 00:03:35,321
Next time, we'll explore
price discrimination.
